In the week ending November 21, the advance figure for seasonally adjusted initial claims was 189,000, a decrease of
13,000 from the previous week's revised level. The previous week's level was revised down by 1,000 from 203,000 to
202,000. The 4-week moving average was 196,000, a decrease of 1,500 from the previous week's revised average. The
previous week's average was revised down by 250 from 197,750 to 197,500.
The advance seasonally adjusted insured unemployment rate was 1.1 percent for the week ending November 14, a
decrease of 0.1 percentage point from the previous week's revised rate. The previous week's rate was revised up by 0.1
from 1.1 to 1.2 percent. The advance number for seasonally adjusted insured unemployment during the week ending
November 14 was 1,697,000, a decrease of 95,000 from the previous week's revised level. The previous week's level was
revised up 29,000 from 1,763,000 to 1,792,000. The 4-week moving average was 1,724,750, an increase of 500 from the
previous week's revised average. The previous week's average was revised up by 7,250 from 1,717,000 to 1,724,250.

2

UNADJUSTED DATA
The advance number of actual initial claims under state programs, unadjusted, totaled 212,945 in the week ending
November 21, an increase of 15,894 (or 8.1 percent) from the previous week. The seasonal factors had expected an
increase of 30,549 (or 15.5 percent) from the previous week. There were 244,713 initial claims in the comparable week
in 2025.
The advance unadjusted insured unemployment rate was 1.0 percent during the week ending November 14, unchanged
from the prior week. The advance unadjusted level of insured unemployment in state programs totaled 1,562,814, a
decrease of 5,578 (or -0.4 percent) from the preceding week. The seasonal factors had expected an increase of 82,453
(or 5.3 percent) from the previous week. A year earlier the rate was 1.2 percent and the volume was 1,781,543.

The total number of continued weeks claimed for benefits in all programs for the week ending November 7 was
1,593,243, an increase of 68,593 from the previous week. There were 1,765,719 weekly claims filed for benefits in all
programs in the comparable week in 2025.
No state was triggered "on" the Extended Benefits program during the week ending November 7.
Initial claims for UI benefits filed by former Federal civilian employees totaled 554 in the week ending November 14, a
decrease of 178 from the prior week. There were 379 initial claims filed by newly discharged veterans, a decrease of 77
from the preceding week.
There were 5,350 continued weeks claimed filed by former Federal civilian employees the week ending November 7, a
decrease of 55 from the previous week. Newly discharged veterans claiming benefits totaled 4,876, an increase of 14
from the prior week.

3
The highest insured unemployment rates in the week ending November 7 were in New Jersey (2.1), Washington (2.0),
Massachusetts (1.8), California (1.7), Michigan (1.6), Nevada (1.6), Oregon (1.6), Puerto Rico (1.6), Rhode Island (1.6),
and Alaska (1.5).
The largest increases in initial claims for the week ending November 14 were in Michigan (+6,036), Wisconsin (+210),
Kentucky (+106), Minnesota (+101), and Oklahoma (+87), while the largest decreases were in Missouri (-3,286), New
Jersey (-2,717), Texas (-2,069), California (-1,787), and North Carolina (-1,357).
